  "live_records": {
    "q-120267": {
      "dom_id": "step-q120267",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "The price $P,$ in dollars, of electricity in a certain city is directly proportional to the consumption $C,$ measured in kilowatt-hour ($\\text{kWh}).$ When the consumption is $C=24\\,\\text{kWh},$ the price is $P=$264.$ What is the constant of variation?",
      "worked_solution": "The price $P$ is directly proportional to the consumption $C,$ so we have\n\n$$\nP=kC,\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that when the consumption is $C=24\\,\\text{kWh},$ the price is $P=$264.$ We can use this information to solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}264 & =k\\cdot (24) \\\\ 264 & =24k \\\\ \\frac{264}{24} & =k \\\\ 11 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $k=\\frac{P}{C},$ we have that the units of $k$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{dollars}}{\\text{kWh}} & =\\text{dollars per kilowatt-hour}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our final answer is $k=$11$ per kilowatt-hour.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
