    "q-210541": {
      "dom_id": "step-q210541",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "The volume of space $V$ a particular gas occupies, measured in liters $(L),$ is directly proportional to its temperature $T,$ measured in Kelvin $(\\text{K}).$ The volume of the gas is $V=48\\,\\text{L}$ when its temperature is $T=360\\,\\text{K}.$ Find the volume of the gas when its temperature is $300\\,\\text{K}.$\n\nThe volume of the gas is\n{{field-1}}$\\text{L}.$",
      "worked_solution": "The volume of the gas can be modeled as a direct variation\n\n$$\nV=kT,\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that the gas occupies $V=48\\,\\text{L}$ at $T=360\\,\\text{K}.$ We can use this information to solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}48 & =k\\cdot 360 \\\\ \\frac{48}{360} & =k \\\\ \\frac{2}{15} & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have the equation\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{2}{15}T.\n$$\n\nWe can use this equation to find the volume occupied by the gas when $T=300\\,\\text{K}$:\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{2}{15}\\cdot 300=40\n$$\n\nTherefore, when the temperature of the gas is $300\\,\\text{K},$ its volume is $40\\,\\text{L}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "field-1",
          "type": "blank",
