    "q-116379": {
      "dom_id": "step-q116379",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "The amount of bleach $b,$ measured in gallons ($gal$), in a certain detergent product varies directly with the amount of water $w,$ measured in gallons, in the product. When the amount of bleach is $b=1.25\\,gal,$ the amount of water is $w=3.75\\,gal.$ Find the amount of bleach when the amount of water is $w=9\\,gal.$",
      "worked_solution": "The amount of bleach $b$ is directly proportional to the amount of water $w,$ so we have\n\n$$\nb=kw.\n$$\n\nWe are told that when the amount of bleach is $b=1.25\\,gal,$ the amount of water is $w=3.75\\,gal.$ We can use this information to solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1.25 & =k\\cdot (3.75) \\\\ 1.25 & =3.75k \\\\ \\frac{1.25}{3.75} & =k \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have the equation\n\n$$\nb=\\frac{1}{3}w.\n$$\n\nWe can use this equation to find the amount of bleach when the amount of water is  $w=9\\,gal,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nb=\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (9)=3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the amount of bleach when $w=9\\,gal$ is $b=3\\,\\text{gal}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
