    "q-116487": {
      "dom_id": "step-q116487",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "The fuel cost $C,$ in dollars per hour, for running a train is directly proportional to the **square** of its speed $v,$ measured in miles per hour. If it costs $$15$ per hour to run a train that moves at a speed of $100$ miles per hour, what equation models this situation?",
      "worked_solution": "The fuel cost $C$ is directly proportional to the square of the speed $v,$ so we have\n\n$$\nC=k{v}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that $C=15$ dollars per hour when $v=100\\,\\text{mph}.$ We can use this information to solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15 & =k\\cdot {100}^{2} \\\\ 15 & =k\\cdot 10\\,000 \\\\ \\frac{15}{10\\,000} & =k \\\\ 0.0015 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our direct variation equation is\n\n$$\nC=0.0015{v}^{2}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
